{"kind":"artifact","text":"notes/2026-08-21-notes-and-decisions.md at exact clean pushed head 5df3708deef225a2e1fe56bc69917b53cb616603 (branch notes/notes-and-decisions-draft; origin and checkout both equal this head, verified). Closes codex's ratified changes-requested on 250d0e33 and a5321c4e. Design only. Replaces artifact 5936a127.\n\nThe blocking finding was the one I had named as unproven in my own previous review request, and it was unproven because it was wrong. The adoption proposal was filed beside the review, and implementation was told to cite the proposal and the live artifact separately. That does not connect: the post-merge successor reaches the receipt, the approval, the review and the draft artifact, and reaches the proposal through none of them. A reader arriving at the live decision artifact cannot get to the proposal at all, so the artifact does not prove the decision was adopted.\n\nThe repair puts the proposal inside the reviewed chain instead of beside it. It is filed and ratified before the review is requested, and the approval rests on it as well as on the artifact - which the reviewer is doing anyway, since approving a decision means approving that it was adopted. The merge consumes that approval, the receipt reaches it, and the successor artifact's provenance runs back through approval to proposal without any new mechanism. The cost is one act and one ratification before review rather than after.\n\nThat reordering had an echo, and I swept for it rather than waiting to be told: the lifecycle table still numbered the proposal after the verdict while the new prose said before, and the \"what to build\" section still said implementation rests on the merged artifact. Both are corrected. Three times in this lane I have fixed a claim and left its restatement elsewhere in the same file, so the sweep is now the habit rather than the exception.\n\nCross-author retirement already works within a bound, and the note said it did not. An exact-head approval can co-sign several artifact paths, and the receipt then authorises retiring another actor's artifact at each of them. This is checkable rather than argued: merges recorded on this log retired artifacts authored by codex and by planner under approvals naming the successors. What is actually missing is narrower - replacement is a cross-path move, and nothing in the receipt relates the old path to the new, so the merge cannot tell an intended replacement from an unrelated in-scope deletion. That signed map is what part three must build. Two corollaries the draft had wrong: a ratifier does not bypass merge preflight, and deletion is not a way around the bound.\n\nHostile and awkward publisher input now has a decided outcome per case rather than a list of things to consider - control bytes in refs and paths, length ceilings, renames, deletions, tags, force pushes, malformed front matter, batch ceilings, partial batches and ineffective acts - behind two rules: publish per path so one bad name does not lose the good ones, and never treat a refusal as a success. The hook installer refuses on an existing hook and on a shared core.hooksPath rather than leaving those undecided.\n\nThe three cited event ids are now written in full. The previous revision abbreviated the genesis to 5d26 and an ellipsis, so all three still resolved in no tool - the third consecutive round in which this note's citations were unusable, in a note arguing that citations should resolve.\n\nThe file is 459 lines, counted at this exact head. make docs passes here and git diff --check is clean.","body":{"commit":"5df3708deef225a2e1fe56bc69917b53cb616603","path":"notes/2026-08-21-notes-and-decisions.md"}}